73:def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
74:    """
75:    Compress the stacked colored bars into a compact ordered table with the control pixel on the selected row.
76:
77:    Input structure
78:    - A background grid contains several full-width colored bars from top to
79:      bottom; the last bar is the base/separator color.
80:    - Each non-base bar may have an aligned three-column comb motif below it,
81:      alternating side-pair rows with solid rows.
82:    - A singleton control pixel sits away from the colored motifs.
83:
84:    Rule
85:    - Make one output row per non-base bar, preserving top-to-bottom order.
86:    - In that row, paint the bar color once for each solid three-pixel row in
87:      its motif, then paint the base-bar color in the separator column.
88:    - The singleton control pixel sits immediately above the base bar. Among
89:      motifs whose open side-pair parity is compatible with that bottom row,
90:      put the control pixel in the output row with the fewest solid rows; use
91:      horizontal distance to the motif center only to break count ties.
92:
93:    Key insight
94:    - The vertical comb height is a count, while the control pixel is read by
95:      projecting it onto the center gap of compatible comb rows rather than by
96:      its vertical band alone.
97:    """
98:    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
99:    bg = get_bg(g)
100:    bars = _full_width_bars(g, bg)
101:    source_bars = bars[:-1]
102:    base_color = bars[-1][1]
103:    base_r = bars[-1][0]
104:    n_rows = len(source_bars)
105:
